From 011f4a32d6c4879d577676db27b170aebf9eb451 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 15:07:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ram: generate_regfile, a multi-port register file as placed
 standard cells
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

generate_regfile builds a register file with many read and write ports
over few words as an array of placed standard cells, from a spec that
names the module, its words and bits, its read and write ports by the
RTL's own port names, and the cells to use:

    generate_regfile -spec rf.regfile -check_ports rf.v \
      -verilog rf_cells.v -def rf.def -lef rf.lef -liberty rf.lib

The array is laid out as a bitcell array is: word rows by bit columns,
each tile a flop, its write select and an AND per read port; the read
word lines are decoded in a header column, the read bit lines are OR
trees in a footer row. Every cell is placed by construction. Banks,
bit folds, a registered read and a word that reads zero are spec
options.

It writes the cells as structural Verilog, their placement as DEF, an
abstract LEF and a timing model (.lib, and a _pre_layout.lib). In
`mode netlist` the address decode is left unplaced for the parent to
place and size, and the abstract's pins sit where their connections
land in the array, so a macro placer that flips the block sees where
its wires go. -check_ports refuses a spec whose ports are not the
module's.

A flow can use it as a macro, or place the macro and then replace it
with the cells, as ORFS's AUTO_MEMORIES_REGFILES does.

Test: src/ram/test/generate_regfile_asap7, an 8 x 4, two-bank, 2R2W
file with word 0 reading zero, checked against RTL that declares its
ports several to a line; its four views against golden files.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
